<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Falémé River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Falémé is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, a left-bank tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, that rises in the Fouta Djallon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and forms much of the border between Senegal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>. Its valley runs through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambuk">Bambuk</a>, one of the great gold-producing regions of medieval and early modern West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/faleme-river.jpg" alt="Falémé River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TomásPGil · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C3%8DO_FAL%C3%89M%C3%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
